1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:55,200
Hello. My name is Abdurrahmaan Ali, and today I'm presenting RentalPro, a Digital Rental Management System built as my IT401 capstone project. Over the next few minutes I'll walk through the problem this system solves, how it was designed and built, and the results of testing it — including a live demo of the working application. All six project deliverables — requirements, design, the working prototype, the full project report, 100% feature completion, and the final demo and presentation package — are finished, tested, and documented. Since then, I've also extended the system with a full billing engine, and made it more usable for Harare landlords and tenants, including a choice of English, Shona and Arabic. Most recently, I translated every screen, message and document into Arabic and hardened the system for production — I'll cover all three.

2
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:35,600
Small landlords and property managers are still running their business on spreadsheets, paper leases, and sticky notes. That creates four real problems: there's no single source of truth for rent and lease data; nothing proactively catches missed rent or an expiring lease; cash payments and verbal maintenance requests leave no accountability trail; and tenants have no way to self-serve — they have to call or email for a balance or a repair update. RentalPro is built for five kinds of users on one platform: Property Owners, Property Managers, Tenants, Maintenance Staff, and Administrators — replacing the spreadsheet with one accountable system.

3
00:01:35,600 --> 00:02:24,800
Five objectives guided the whole project. First, centralize property and lease data into one record instead of scattered spreadsheets. Second, automate the reminders landlords actually miss — lease expiry, rent due, overdue rent, and overdue maintenance all fire from background jobs, not a human remembering. Third, give every one of the five roles a scoped view, enforced by role-based access control plus ownership checks — one Owner literally cannot query another Owner's data. Fourth, make every record auditable: payments are immutable, and every state change writes to an append-only audit log. And fifth — the one I'll spend the most time proving today — ship it fully tested. Not just working, but 100% of the requirements implemented and covered by an automated test.

4
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:54,400
Deliverable 1 was the Software Requirements Specification — 25 use cases across six functional areas: authentication and RBAC, property and unit management, the full tenant lifecycle from onboarding to self-service, rent and payments including automated alerts, the six-stage maintenance lifecycle, and reporting. Every one of these 25 use cases — I'll show this again in the testing section — ended up with a route, a template, and at least one automated test behind it.

5
00:02:54,400 --> 00:03:38,400
Deliverable 2 turned those 25 use cases into a UML class diagram and a 13-table relational schema. Every table uses a UUID string primary key instead of an auto-increment integer, specifically so the identical schema runs unmodified on SQLite in development and test, and PostgreSQL in production. A few other decisions from this design carried straight through into the build: naive UTC timestamps to avoid timezone bugs across environments, soft status fields instead of physical deletes so history is never lost, rent payments that are only ever inserted and never edited, and JSON columns on the audit log so any entity's before-and-after state can be captured without a schema migration.

6
00:03:38,400 --> 00:05:00,800
The application is a layered Flask monolith — deliberately simplified from Deliverable 2's more ambitious microservice sketch, because a single developer needed to build and verify it in the time available. Presentation is 40 Jinja2 templates behind a role-aware top navigation bar, with a language menu for English, Shona and Arabic. The application layer is 10 Flask Blueprints — one each for auth, properties, tenants, rent, maintenance, admin, reports, the tenant portal, and the REST API. Below that, the domain and service layer holds security.py for RBAC and IDOR protection, web_security.py for the security headers and the secret-key check, the five scheduler jobs, a small translation module for Shona and Arabic, which also turns Arabic pages right to left, and a services package for PDF generation, Excel export, notifications, and payments. Data is 18 SQLAlchemy models — the 13 from the design plus 5 for the billing engine — with UUID keys, and the schema is managed by Alembic migrations. And infrastructure is Docker Compose running PostgreSQL and Redis, with uploads kept on local disk or in an S3-compatible bucket, with a dev-mode fallback for every paid external service — which is what let me build and test this entire system without a single paid account.

7
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:42,000
Every screen in RentalPro is scoped to one of five roles. Owners create and edit properties and units, configure late fees, and see the full portfolio report. Managers work within properties an Owner already added — onboarding and, new in Deliverable 5, blacklisting tenants, and assigning maintenance work. Tenants get a self-service portal: pay rent online, and submit maintenance requests with photos. Staff see only their own assigned work queue — nothing else in the system is visible to them. And Administrators get full data visibility plus, again new in Deliverable 5, a dedicated console for managing every user's role and account status.

8
00:05:42,000 --> 00:06:49,200
Deliverable 5 is where the project went from working to 100% complete against the original requirements. An audit found eleven gaps, grouped on this slide into eight areas: photo uploads for properties, units, and maintenance tickets, all validated by their actual file content, not just the extension; editing a unit after it's created; configurable late fees, fixed or percentage, applied automatically once rent is overdue; tenant blacklisting, which blocks a future lease without touching an existing one; tenant ID documents and lease document downloads; the admin user console, with a safety guard that won't let you remove the last active Owner; self-service staff account creation; and one-click CSV and Excel exports. All eleven are covered by a dedicated 31-test module, which I'll show numbers for next. When I later traced all 44 functional requirements one by one, two were only partly met — the full tenant history of each unit, and a maintenance summary by period — so I built both, and all 44 requirements are now met.

9
00:06:49,200 --> 00:07:50,800
Beyond the original five deliverables, I extended RentalPro with a full billing engine — eight more capabilities, each additive so the existing 124 tests never had to change. Operational and sundry charges bill one-off costs straight to a lease. Electricity metering bills consumption automatically from a configured rate. Pro rata rent prorates a partial first month instead of charging it in full. Variable rent lets a lease's rate change mid-term — an annual escalation — while past periods stay billed at their own rate. A daily scheduler job now bills each period automatically and idempotently. Account statements support a real date range with an opening and closing balance, and tenants can view their own. Every payment gets a real downloadable PDF receipt. And property expenses now feed a true net income estimate, not just rent collected minus maintenance. Seventy-nine new tests back all of it, and they're included in the testing numbers coming up.

10
00:07:50,800 --> 00:09:12,400
The most recent round of work was about the people who would actually use RentalPro — landlords, managers and tenants in Harare. Every property can now carry map coordinates, set by clicking a map, and there's a portfolio map showing all of them. The interface moved from a sidebar to a horizontal top bar, and the login page and each section of the app now show photographs of Harare. The biggest change is language: a menu in the top bar and on the login page offers English, Shona and Arabic, and the choice sticks when you log in and out. My SRS originally described Arabic and English; both are now in, and because the users are in Harare, I added Shona as well. Arabic is written right to left, so an Arabic page mirrors the whole layout. Only interface text is translated — names and places stay exactly as entered, and a test fails if that ever changes. Tenants now see their unit as 'Avondale Heights, Unit 101' rather than an internal code, a blank-page bug for signed-in users is fixed, and the demo data is now a realistic Harare portfolio: five properties, sixteen units and nine tenants. Twenty-seven new tests cover this work.

11
00:09:12,400 --> 00:10:24,000
The last round of work finished two things: the language support, and readiness for production. Before it, Arabic covered the main screens only. Now every page, form option and message is in Arabic — over six hundred phrases — and a test fails if any new text is added without a translation. Emails and SMS are written in each user's saved language, even when a nightly job sends them. PDFs are fully Arabic too: the letters are joined and read right to left, and tables are mirrored, while names, dates and amounts keep their own order. Every amount in the system now shows its currency. On the production side, every response carries strict security headers — a content policy with no inline code at all — the session cookie is locked down, and the app simply refuses to start with a missing or published secret key. Alembic migrations replace create_all, and existing databases are adopted with their data intact. And uploads can be kept in an S3-compatible bucket, so they survive a redeploy. Fifty-four new tests cover this round.

12
00:10:24,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Here's what that looks like. On the left is the login page: a full-screen photograph of the Harare skyline, a translucent sign-in card, and the language menu open in the corner, offering English, ChiShona and Arabic. On the right is a tenant's dashboard in Arabic. The whole layout reads right to left — the navigation starts on the right, and the cards and tables are mirrored — with a welcome, her unit, her balance, and the button to pay rent. Her name and 'Avondale Heights' stay exactly as entered, because they're data, not interface text.

13
00:11:02,000 --> 00:12:06,800
This is the slide that backs up every claim in this presentation. 298 automated tests, zero failures, 85 percent statement coverage across the backend, and all 25 use cases from the SRS covered by at least one test. The chart breaks that down by module — 36 validation tests, 31 for Deliverable 5's feature gaps, 79 for the billing engine alone, the single largest block in the project, 22 for the property map and the three languages, 13 for the tenant history and maintenance summary screens that completed the last two requirements, and 54 for the final round — full Arabic, security headers, migrations, currency and cloud storage. Importantly, these aren't unit tests calling isolated functions — they go through Flask's real test client, issuing actual HTTP requests through session handling, CSRF, RBAC, and the ORM. That's how a real security bug like cross-owner data access — one Property Owner querying another Owner's property — gets caught before it ships, not after.

14
00:12:06,800 --> 00:13:11,200
Now let's see it running. I'll walk through six things: first, an Owner setting up a property and unit with photos and a configured late fee. Second, onboarding a new tenant, uploading their lease, and watching the unit activate. Third, the Deliverable 5 blacklist toggle and recording a rent payment. Fourth, a full maintenance ticket lifecycle rotating through four different role logins — tenant submits, owner assigns, staff completes, tenant confirms. Fifth, the admin console for user management and creating a new staff account. And finally, the billing engine end to end — charges, metering, pro rata, a rent revision, the automated billing job's paper trail, a filtered account statement, a downloaded payment receipt, and a property expense feeding into net income — and to finish, the Harare portfolio on the map and the whole interface switching to Shona, then to Arabic, laid out right to left, and back to English. [Switch to the recorded demo video or live application here.]

15
00:13:11,200 --> 00:14:05,200
Three challenges stand out. The first was reconciling earlier, more ambitious design documents with what one developer could actually build and verify in the time available — I simplified Deliverable 2's microservice-style sketch into a layered monolith, and when I found Deliverable 3's original numbers had drifted from reality, I corrected the document rather than leaving the gap in place. Second, getting recurring billing correct at calendar edges — a lease starting on the 31st, or a due day that doesn't exist in February — meant writing the edge-case tests before trusting the calculation, not after. Third, testing payments, email, and SMS without a live paid account of any kind — solved with a dev-mode fallback pattern applied consistently across every external integration, which is also exactly what let this whole demo run offline today.

16
00:14:05,200 --> 00:15:15,600
Security wasn't bolted on — these controls run through every request. Passwords are hashed with 600,000 rounds of PBKDF2, and the app refuses to start without a real secret key, since a published one would let anyone forge a login. Every protected route carries a role check, and every sensitive lookup also carries an ownership check at the resource level — the combination is what stops a second Property Owner from viewing the first Owner's data even though they share a role. CSRF protection covers every form, with one deliberate, documented exemption for the Stripe webhook, which instead carries its own manual HMAC verification with a replay window. The session cookie can't be read by scripts or sent on cross-site posts. Every response carries a strict content security policy — no inline code at all — blocks framing, and enables HSTS over HTTPS. Five failed logins locks an account for 15 minutes. Every file upload is validated by its actual magic bytes, not its claimed extension. And every state-changing action writes to an immutable audit log.

17
00:15:15,600 --> 00:16:22,000
To wrap up: RentalPro went from a requirements document, through design, to a working prototype, a full seven-chapter project report, 100% feature completion, an extended billing engine, a localised interface for Harare, and finally full Arabic and production hardening — and every number on this slide was produced by actually running the code, not estimated. 25 of 25 use cases, each with a route and a covering test. The 13-table design implemented exactly as specified. 298 tests, zero failures, 85 percent coverage. Eleven feature gaps closed in Deliverable 5, eight more billing capabilities added since, and an interface in English, Shona and Arabic — with Arabic reaching every screen, message, email and PDF. And the whole thing runs fully offline, with zero paid external accounts required to build or test it. That discipline — that a claim in the report is a fact about the system, not a target for it — is, I'd argue, as much a deliverable of this capstone as the application itself.

18
00:16:22,000 --> 00:17:17,200
Looking ahead to Part 2 of the capstone. First, native-speaker review: the Arabic now covers everything, and Shona covers the main screens, so the next step is for Arabic and Shona speakers to check the wording and for Shona to reach the rest of the app. Second, a mobile-optimized tenant portal, since tenants are the role most likely to use this from a phone. Third, a live production deployment: HTTPS with secure cookies, PostgreSQL with the new migrations, and uploads in an S3 bucket — every piece is supported and tested offline, but hasn't yet run live. Fourth, end-to-end verification against live Stripe, SendGrid, and Twilio sandbox accounts, on top of the offline test suite that's carried the project so far. And last, two-factor sign-in for Owners, Managers and Administrators, the roles that can see the most data.

19
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:40,400
Thank you. That's RentalPro — a fully-implemented, fully-tested digital rental management system covering all six deliverables from requirements through the final demo and presentation package, plus an extended billing engine on top. I'm happy to take any questions — about a specific design decision, the test suite, the security model, or anything you'd like to see demonstrated again.
